---
title: "Topics"
description: "Categorize events with topics and control which destinations receive which events."
---

Topics (also known as event types) allow you to categorize events and route them to matching destinations. Each destination subscribes to one or more topics, and only receives events published under those topics.

Topics determine:
- Which topics destinations can subscribe to
- Valid values for the `topic` field in the [Publish Event](/docs/outpost/api) API
- Topics displayed in the tenant portal

> Topics are optional and if none are set then the `topic` field should be undefined when publishing events and creating destinations.

## Configuring Available Topics

{% tabs tabGroup="deployment" %}
{% tab label="Managed" %}

Available topics are configured through the Hookdeck dashboard [project settings](https://dashboard.hookdeck.com/settings/project/general) or Config API. Topics determine which values are valid for the `topic` field when publishing events and which topics appear in the tenant portal.

Wildcard topic subscriptions are disabled by default. Enable them in [project settings](https://dashboard.hookdeck.com/settings/project/general) or through the Config API with `TOPICS_ALLOW_WILDCARDS=true`.

{% /tab %}
{% tab label="Self-Hosted" %}
Set the list of available topics using the `TOPICS` environment variable:

```
TOPICS=user.created,user.updated,user.deleted,order.placed,order.shipped
```

If `TOPICS` is not set, Outpost defaults to `*`, allowing any topic value.

Wildcard topic subscriptions are disabled by default. Set `TOPICS_ALLOW_WILDCARDS=TRUE` to allow `*` inside destination topic strings.
{% /tab %}
{% /tabs %}

When wildcard topic subscriptions are disabled, Outpost ignores stored wildcard patterns during matching and omits them from destination and tenant API responses. The stored topics are not changed, so re-enabling wildcard subscriptions restores the patterns. Exact topic subscriptions and the standalone `"*"` subscribe-to-all value continue to work.

## Destination Topic Subscriptions

Each destination subscribes to one or more topics. A destination receives an event only if:
1. The destination is enabled
2. The event's topic matches one of the destination's subscribed topics
3. The event matches the destination's filter (if any)

Subscribe to all topics with the wildcard `"*"`:

```sh
curl '{% $OUTPOST_API_BASE_URL %}/tenants/<TENANT_ID>/destinations' \
--header 'Content-Type: application/json' \
--header 'Authorization: Bearer <API_KEY>' \
--data '{
  "type": "webhook",
  "topics": ["*"],
  "config": { "url": "https://example.com/webhooks" }
}'
```

Subscribe to specific topics:

```sh
curl '{% $OUTPOST_API_BASE_URL %}/tenants/<TENANT_ID>/destinations' \
--header 'Content-Type: application/json' \
--header 'Authorization: Bearer <API_KEY>' \
--data '{
  "type": "webhook",
  "topics": ["order.placed", "order.shipped"],
  "config": { "url": "https://example.com/order-webhooks" }
}'
```

When wildcard topic subscriptions are enabled, destination topics can also use `*` inside a topic string as a wildcard that matches any run of characters. For example, `user.*` matches `user.created` and `user.profile.updated`, `*.created` matches `user.created` and `order.created`, and `order.*.completed` matches `order.payment.completed`.

When available topics are configured, wildcard patterns must match at least one available topic.

## Event Fanout

A single published event is independently delivered to every destination that matches its topic. Each delivery attempt is tracked separately.

## Tenant topics subscriptions

The tenant object's `topics` array lists all topics currently in use. 
This is useful for keeping track of the complete set of topics a tenant is subscribed to across all their destinations, and can help you avoid unnecessary event publishing.

```sh
curl '{% $OUTPOST_API_BASE_URL %}/tenants/<TENANT_ID>' \
--header 'Authorization: Bearer <API_KEY>'
```

```json
{
  "id": "org_acme",
  "topics": ["user.created", "order.placed"]
  [...]
}
```

You can also subscribe to the `tenant.subscription.updated` [Operator Events](/docs/outpost/features/operator-events) topic to receive an event when the tenant's subscribed topics change.

## Topic Schemas

A topic schema describes the events of one topic: what they represent, a JSON Schema for their `data`, whether Outpost validates published events against it, and whether the topic is exposed to [MCP Events](/docs/outpost/guides/mcp-events) clients. Schemas are optional and set per topic. A topic without one keeps working unchanged.

In API v2, [`GET /topics`](#listing-topics) returns each topic with its schema, so you can document your event types once and render them in your own UI.

### Topic Object

```json
{
  "name": "order.created",
  "description": "Fires when a new order is placed.",
  "payload_schema": {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "order_id": { "type": "string", "x-mcp-filter": false },
      "total": { "type": "number", "description": "Order total in major units." },
      "currency": { "type": "string", "description": "ISO 4217 currency code." },
      "created_at": { "type": "string", "format": "date-time", "x-mcp-filter": false }
    },
    "required": ["order_id", "total", "currency"]
  },
  "validation": "enforce",
  "mcp": { "enabled": true }
}
```

| Field | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| `name` | Optional. When set, it must equal the topic name the schema is keyed by. |
| `description` | What the topic's events represent. |
| `payload_schema` | A JSON Schema 2020-12 object for the event `data`. See [Schema Support](#schema-support). |
| `validation` | `off` (the default), `warn` or `enforce`. `warn` and `enforce` need a `payload_schema`. See [Validation](#validation). |
| `mcp.enabled` | Exposes the topic to MCP Events clients. Defaults to `false`. Needs a `payload_schema` whose root `type` is `object`. See [MCP Subscription Arguments](#mcp-subscription-arguments). |
| `deprecated` | Marks the topic as deprecated. See [Schema Evolution and Deprecation](#schema-evolution-and-deprecation). |
| `replaced_by` | The topic that replaces a deprecated topic. Needs `deprecated: true`. |

Unknown fields are rejected, so a typo such as `validaton` fails startup instead of being ignored.

### Configuring Topic Schemas

{% tabs tabGroup="deployment" %}
{% tab label="Managed" %}

Topic schemas aren't configurable on managed Outpost yet.

{% /tab %}
{% tab label="Self-Hosted" %}

Schemas are keyed by topic name and come from these settings:

| Variable | YAML key | Contents |
|----------|----------|----------|
| `TOPICS_SCHEMAS` | `topics_schemas` | Schemas as a JSON object. In a YAML config file, a mapping or a JSON string. |
| `TOPICS_SCHEMAS_FILE` | `topics_schemas_file` | Path to a YAML or JSON file with the same content. |
| `TOPICS_SCHEMAS_OPENAPI` | `topics_schemas_openapi` | An OpenAPI 3.1 document to import schemas from, as a file path or URL. See [Importing from OpenAPI](#importing-from-openapi). |

`TOPICS_SCHEMAS` and `TOPICS_SCHEMAS_FILE` are mutually exclusive. Either one can be combined with `TOPICS_SCHEMAS_OPENAPI`: its entry for a topic replaces the imported entry as a whole.

A file keeps schemas in your repository, next to the code that publishes the events:

```yaml
# topics-schemas.yaml
order.created:
  description: Fires when a new order is placed.
  validation: enforce
  mcp:
    enabled: true
  payload_schema:
    type: object
    properties:
      order_id: { type: string, x-mcp-filter: false }
      total: { type: number, description: Order total in major units. }
      currency: { type: string, description: ISO 4217 currency code. }
    required: [order_id, total, currency]
```

```
TOPICS=order.created,order.shipped
TOPICS_SCHEMAS_FILE=/config/outpost/topics-schemas.yaml
```

YAML is read with YAML 1.2 core types, and dates and timestamps stay strings, so `default: 2026-01-01` keeps its value. Docker Compose interpolates `$` in environment values: write `$$ref` and `$$defs` in a Compose file, or use `TOPICS_SCHEMAS_FILE`.

Only the API service loads schemas, once at startup, including when a single process runs every service. The delivery and log services, `outpost migrate` and `outpost config list` don't load them, so in a split deployment only the API service needs the file or URL, and a problem in the schemas, including in a `topics_schemas` mapping in the YAML config file, only stops the API service. Restart the API service to apply a change. If the configuration is invalid, the API service fails to start and lists the problems at once, up to 100. Startup warnings are listed the same way.

{% /tab %}
{% /tabs %}

Each schema key must match a topic in `TOPICS` exactly. A key that only differs by whitespace fails with a suggestion. Wildcard keys such as `*` or `user.*` aren't allowed, and schemas need `TOPICS` to be set.

### Importing from OpenAPI

If an OpenAPI 3.1 document already describes your webhooks in its top-level `webhooks` section, Outpost can import schemas from it. The `post` operation of each entry becomes a topic:

| OpenAPI | Topic field |
|---------|-------------|
| `x-outpost-topic` on the operation or path item, else the `webhooks` key | `name` |
| The operation's `summary`, else its `description` | `description` |
| The request body schema for `application/json`, else for the first `+json` media type | `payload_schema` |
| `x-mcp-enabled: true` on the operation | `mcp.enabled` |
| `deprecated: true` on the operation | `deprecated` |
| `x-mcp-filter` on properties | Kept in `payload_schema` |

- References into `#/components` are followed at the path item, operation, request body and media type levels. Schemas referenced from `#/components/schemas` are copied into the payload schema's `$defs`, so each payload schema stands alone. A reference to another file or URL fails startup for a topic in `TOPICS`. When the path item or operation itself is in another file, the topic is the `webhooks` key, or the `x-outpost-topic` next to the reference.
- Imported topics have `validation: off`. To validate one, or to set its `replaced_by`, define it in `TOPICS_SCHEMAS` or `TOPICS_SCHEMAS_FILE`, which replaces the imported entry as a whole.
- Webhooks whose topic isn't in `TOPICS` are skipped with a startup warning, even when Outpost couldn't import them, so the document can describe more webhooks than you send through Outpost. A problem that hides the topic name still fails startup: an invalid `x-outpost-topic`, or a reference to a path item or operation in the document that doesn't resolve, unless `x-outpost-topic` next to the reference names the topic. Keys in `TOPICS_SCHEMAS` and `TOPICS_SCHEMAS_FILE` must still be in `TOPICS`.
- The document is JSON or YAML, up to 4 MiB and 1,000,000 values, with each `$ref` up to 4 KiB. OpenAPI 3.0 isn't supported.
- A problem in an object that several webhooks reference is reported once, with the number of webhooks that reference it.

`TOPICS_SCHEMAS_OPENAPI` can be an `http` or `https` URL. It's fetched on every API service startup, with a 10 second timeout and up to 3 redirects, never from HTTPS to HTTP. A change to the remote document then changes your topics on the next restart without a deploy, API instances that restart at different times can run different schemas, and an unreachable host stops the API service from starting.

We recommend vendoring the document: commit a copy next to your configuration and set `TOPICS_SCHEMAS_OPENAPI` to its file path. If you use a URL, pin it with `TOPICS_SCHEMAS_OPENAPI_SHA256`, the hex SHA-256 of the document (for example, the output of `sha256sum openapi.yaml`). A document that doesn't match fails startup instead of changing your topics. Outpost logs the SHA-256 of the document it loads at startup.

### Schema Support

`payload_schema` is a [JSON Schema 2020-12](https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12) object, with these rules:

- `$schema` must be absent or `https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema`.
- `$ref` and `$dynamicRef` must be JSON Pointers to a schema within `payload_schema`, such as `#/$defs/address`, and not into another value, such as an `x-` extension or a `default`. Anchor references, such as `#address`, aren't supported, and Outpost never fetches remote schemas.
- `$id` is only allowed at the root of `payload_schema`, since a nested `$id` changes how references below it resolve.
- `format` is checked for `date` and `date-time` only. Other formats, such as `email`, `uri` or `uuid`, are annotations and never fail validation.
- Regular expressions in `pattern` and `patternProperties` use [RE2 syntax](https://github.com/google/re2/wiki/Syntax), which has no lookarounds or backreferences. A pattern RE2 can't compile fails startup when the topic's `validation` is `warn` or `enforce` or its `mcp.enabled` is set. For other topics it's a startup warning: the schema is still returned by `GET /topics` but never used for validation.
- A `payload_schema` can be up to 256 KiB, or 64 KiB when `mcp.enabled` is set.

### Validation

`validation` decides what happens to a published event whose `data` doesn't match `payload_schema`:

| Mode | Invalid `data` |
|------|----------------|
| `off` | Not checked. The default. |
| `warn` | Accepted. The event is stored with `schema_valid: false` and counted in the `outpost.events.schema_invalid` [OpenTelemetry metric](/docs/outpost/features/opentelemetry). Use it to find failing publishers before you enforce. |
| `enforce` | Rejected and counted in the same metric. The Publish API returns `422` with the validation errors. |

Validation runs before the event is matched to destinations, for events from the Publish API and from publish queues. It's instance configuration, not part of the API version, so `enforce` applies to API v1 publishes too.

`data` that repeats a key within an object fails validation, whatever the schema, since JSON parsers disagree on which of the values counts.

```json
{
  "status": 422,
  "message": "validation error",
  "data": [
    "data.total: must be number",
    "data: missing required property \"currency\""
  ]
}
```

Each error is a path from `data`, such as `data.items[2].sku`, and a message. Errors never include published values. At most 20 are listed, sorted, followed by `... and N more`.

Validated events carry a `schema_valid` field in API responses, including events embedded in attempts: `true` when `data` passed, `false` when it failed under `warn`. It's omitted when the event wasn't validated.

Event `data` larger than the validation size limit isn't validated. The limit is 256 KiB by default, set with `TOPICS_VALIDATION_MAX_BYTES` on self-hosted Outpost. With `enforce`, the publish is rejected with `data exceeds the schema validation size limit`. With `warn`, the event is accepted without `schema_valid`. Both count in the metric with reason `too_large`.

Validating `data` takes roughly 20 to 350 times its size in memory, the most for large arrays whose items fail the schema. Each `anyOf` or `oneOf` branch those items fail adds about 80 times more, since the errors of every branch are kept: with a 16-branch `anyOf`, 256 KiB of `data` can take about 330 MiB.

Outpost caps the validations running at once, which keeps that memory bounded under concurrent publishes. Each validation counts the size of its `data` times one plus the branch count of the largest `anyOf` or `oneOf` in the topic's schema (at most 32), and they add up to at most four times the size limit, or about 350 MiB of memory with the default limit. A validation that counts for more than that runs alone, and can take more: about 600 MiB for 256 KiB of `data` against a 32-branch `anyOf`. Publishes to topics that aren't validated never wait. Raise the limit only with memory to match.

A publish queue message that fails `enforce` validation is treated as invalid: Outpost logs its event ID, topic and errors, and rejects it on RabbitMQ and Azure Service Bus. AWS SQS and GCP Pub/Sub have no reject, so the message is redelivered until `PUBLISH_MAX_REDELIVERIES` or the queue's own redelivery limit is reached. See the Failed messages section of [Publish from RabbitMQ](/docs/outpost/publishing/publish-from-rabbitmq#failed-messages).

### MCP Subscription Arguments

When `mcp.enabled` is set, each top-level property of `payload_schema` can be an optional subscription argument. An MCP Events client passes arguments when it subscribes, and only receives events whose `data` matches them.

A property is an argument when its schema, after following local `$ref`s, has a scalar `type` (`string`, `number`, `integer` or `boolean`, optionally with `null`), or a scalar `enum` or `const`. Objects, arrays, and composite schemas without a scalar `type` aren't arguments.

Each argument accepts:

- A value, such as `"currency": "USD"`.
- A list of 1 to 100 values, matching any of them, such as `"currency": ["USD", "EUR"]`.
- For `number` and `integer` properties and strings with `format: date`, range operators: `$gt`, `$gte`, `$lt` and `$lte`, such as `"total": { "$gte": 100 }`.

Strings with `format: date-time` accept values and lists but no range operators. Filters compare strings byte by byte, which doesn't order timestamps with offsets or fractional seconds correctly. String values are at most 256 characters, or the property's `maxLength` when it's lower.

Hide a property from arguments with `"x-mcp-filter": false`, for example for IDs and timestamps that make poor filters. `x-mcp-filter` must be a boolean. Properties whose name starts with `$` are never arguments, since filters would read them as operators. MCP clients receive the payload schema without `x-mcp-filter`.

Outpost publishes the arguments to MCP clients as the event's `inputSchema` in `events/list`, and validates subscriptions against it. Unknown arguments fail validation.

When a client subscribes, Outpost turns its arguments into the subscription's [destination filter](/docs/outpost/features/filter). Each argument becomes a condition on the same property of the event `data`, and an event must meet all of them:

| Argument | Filter |
|----------|--------|
| `"currency": "USD"` | `{"data": {"currency": "USD"}}` |
| `"currency": ["USD", "EUR"]` | `{"data": {"currency": {"$in": ["USD", "EUR"]}}}` |
| `"total": {"$gte": 100}` | `{"data": {"total": {"$gte": 100}}}` |
| `"total": {"$gte": 100}, "currency": "USD"` | `{"data": {"total": {"$gte": 100}, "currency": "USD"}}` |
| `{}` or no arguments | No filter: every event of the topic |

The filter matches the event, not the MCP envelope, so payload fields sit directly under `data`. An MCP server with rules the arguments can't express can pass its own `filter` on subscribe instead. See the [MCP Events guide](/docs/outpost/guides/mcp-events) to set up subscriptions, and [MCP destination](/docs/outpost/destinations/mcp) for how events are delivered.

### Listing Topics

In API v2, `GET /topics` returns every topic in `TOPICS` order, with its schema configuration. A topic without a schema has only `name`, `validation: "off"` and `mcp.enabled: false`. API v1 keeps returning topic names; see [Upgrade to API v2](/docs/outpost/self-hosting/changelog/upgrade-api-v2).

```sh
curl 'http://localhost:3333/api/v2/topics' \
--header 'Authorization: Bearer <API_KEY>'
```

```json
[
  {
    "name": "order.created",
    "description": "Fires when a new order is placed.",
    "payload_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "order_id": { "type": "string", "x-mcp-filter": false },
        "total": { "type": "number", "description": "Order total in major units." }
      },
      "required": ["order_id", "total"]
    },
    "validation": "enforce",
    "mcp": { "enabled": true }
  },
  {
    "name": "order.shipped",
    "validation": "off",
    "mcp": { "enabled": false }
  }
]
```

`payload_schema` is returned as configured, including `x-mcp-filter`.

### Schema Evolution and Deprecation

To make a change that existing consumers can't handle, such as removing a field or changing its type, publish the new version under a new topic and deprecate the old one:

1. Add the new topic, by convention with a version suffix such as `order.created.v2`, and publish to both topics during the overlap.
2. Set `deprecated: true` and `replaced_by: "order.created.v2"` on the old topic. `GET /topics` returns both fields, and MCP clients see the old topic's description prefixed with `Deprecated: use order.created.v2.` Publishing to the old topic and its existing destinations keep working.
3. Remove the old topic from `TOPICS` once its publishers and destinations have moved. Its remaining MCP subscriptions are then ended, as described in [Ending a topic](#ending-a-topic).

`replaced_by` must name another topic in `TOPICS`. If the deprecated topic is MCP-enabled, its replacement must be MCP-enabled too.

#### Breaking-change check

An MCP subscription outlives the event list it was created from: the client refreshes it with the same arguments, and expects payloads that match the schema it saw. So once a topic has live MCP subscriptions, its schema can only change additively.

When the API service starts with topic schemas that differ from the last applied ones, which it keeps in Redis, it compares each topic that is MCP-enabled in both and has at least one live subscription. The check is as lenient as possible while existing subscriptions stay valid:

- **Allowed:** new properties, required or not; changes to `required`; descriptions and other annotations; widenings, such as `integer` to `number`, a new `enum` value or a removed `type`; relaxed constraints.
- **Breaking:** a property removed or renamed, at any depth; a type narrowed, such as `number` to `integer`; an `enum` narrowed or added, or a `const` changed or added; a constraint tightened or added, such as a raised `minimum`, a lowered `maxLength`, a new or changed `pattern` or `format`, or `additionalProperties: false`; a top-level property that is no longer an argument, because of `x-mcp-filter: false` or a type that can't be filtered.
- **Composites:** an `allOf` whose branches are all object schemas is compared as the union of their properties. Any change inside `anyOf`, `oneOf`, `not`, `if`, `then`, `else`, `dependentSchemas`, `patternProperties`, `prefixItems`, `contains` or `unevaluated*` is breaking.

Changes to topics without live subscriptions, or without `mcp.enabled`, aren't checked. Nor is ending a topic, which ends its subscriptions instead.

On a breaking change, the API service fails to start and lists each change: the topic, a JSON pointer into its payload schema, and the kind of change (`property_removed`, `type_narrowed`, `enum_narrowed`, `const_changed`, `constraint_tightened`, `filter_hidden` or `composite_changed`). The supported fix is the new-topic path above.

**Rollouts and rollbacks.** Each API instance reports the topic configuration it runs to Redis every 20 seconds. A configuration that an instance has reported in the last 60 seconds is accepted without a check, and doesn't replace the last applied one. During a rolling deploy, instances on the previous configuration can therefore restart. A rollback to a configuration that no instance runs anymore is checked like any other change, and rolling back a widening is a narrowing.

**Forcing a breaking change.** With `TOPICS_ALLOW_BREAKING_CHANGES=true`, Outpost applies the change and ends the affected topics' subscriptions: every subscription created or last refreshed against an old schema of the topic gets a `terminated` envelope with `Unsupported` and `{"feature": "payloadSchema", "reason": "schema_changed"}`, so clients resubscribe against the new event list. That includes subscriptions that instances still on the old schema accept during the rollout, until `MCP_TTL_MAX` after the last such instance stops. An instance on the new schema doesn't wait for the background pass: a client refreshing such a subscription through it gets the same error in the subscribe response instead of a `terminated` envelope, and the subscription is deleted, so subscribing again creates a new one against the new schema. Set the flag for one deploy, then remove it: Outpost logs a warning while it's set.

#### Ending a topic

A topic ends for MCP when you remove it from `TOPICS` or from your schemas, or turn off its `mcp.enabled`. API instances running that configuration stop delivering the topic's events to MCP subscriptions, and on its next pass, the API service deletes the topic's subscriptions and sends each a `terminated` envelope with `NotFound` and `{"kind": "event"}`. Subscriptions already past their `expires_at` get no envelope: they're deleted as expired.

Subscriptions are only ended when the topic is off in both the instance's configuration and the last applied one, so an instance on an older or rolled-back configuration never ends them.

As a guard against a missing or empty schema configuration, the API service refuses to start with a configuration that ends every MCP-enabled topic at once, unless `TOPICS_ALLOW_BREAKING_CHANGES` is set.

